<%#
  One photo's caption in the homepage strip (see the strip's comment in views/public/index.ejs): the project's name
  and, under it, its category. It is laid over the bottom-left of the photo and belongs inside the photo's wrapper,
  which is the `group` that drives it. `name` and `category` are its two lines. It takes no pointer events, so the
  photo underneath keeps the hover.
  While the pointer is over the photo a dark gradient fades in behind the caption (so the white text reads on any
  photo), the name's letters rise one after another out of a mask, as the headline's do (public/js/loader.js), and
  the category rises out of a mask of its own, as one line, a moment after the name has started. All of it has the
  same ease (power3.out, as a cubic-bezier) and takes 600ms; the letters are staggered by 30ms each and the category
  starts 200ms in. When the pointer leaves everything drops back together, quicker (300ms), with no stagger or
  delay: a transition takes the delay and duration of the state it is heading to, so those live on the hover side.
  With reduced motion there is no transition on the text, so it simply appears with the gradient.
  The name: a transform needs a box, so each letter is its own inline-block span, and the split is made here, not in
  the browser: the CSS does all of the animating and there is no script to wait for. Each letter's place in the
  order is its --i, set below. The spans are aria-hidden and the name is read from a visually hidden copy instead,
  so it is announced as one word, not letter by letter. A space becomes a non-breaking one so that its span keeps
  its width. The mask is the name's own box, tall enough (leading) for the tail of a Q; the letters start a full
  letter height below it. The type is Bevellier, like the wordmark and the headings, in the headline's uppercase
  (inherited from the <h1>), at the photo cursor's fluid size (index.ejs), and the padding follows the window the
  same way. Bevellier is drawn tight, for large sizes: at a caption's size the wordmark's tight tracking makes
  neighbouring capitals touch (TE, RI), so the name gets a little extra (tracking-wide) instead. The classes on the
  letters' parent reach the letters through `*:` (a direct child), so the letters carry only their --i.
  The category: Supreme, the site's text face, medium, in the same uppercase, at half the name's size but never
  below the type scale's text-xs, and a little softer than the name (white at 80%). It is not split into letters, so
  when the box is too narrow for it, it wraps onto a second line, and its mask is as tall as its text.
%>
<span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 flex flex-col items-start justify-end p-[max(0.75rem,1.25vw)] text-white [font-size:clamp(0.9rem,1.5vw,1.9rem)]">
  <span aria-hidden="true" class="absolute inset-0 bg-linear-to-t from-bunker-950/75 to-transparent to-55% opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-500 group-hover:opacity-100"></span>
  <span class="relative block overflow-clip whitespace-nowrap font-bevellier font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-wide">
    <span class="sr-only"><%= name %></span>
    <span aria-hidden="true" class="block *:inline-block *:translate-y-full *:transition-[translate] *:duration-300 *:ease-[cubic-bezier(0.215,0.61,0.355,1)] group-hover:*:translate-y-0 group-hover:*:duration-600 group-hover:*:delay-[calc(var(--i)*30ms)] motion-reduce:*:transition-none"><% [...name].forEach((letter, i) => { %><span style="--i: <%= i %>"><%= letter === ' ' ? ' ' : letter %></span><% }) %></span>
  </span>
  <span class="relative block overflow-clip">
    <span class="block translate-y-full font-supreme font-medium leading-[1.3] tracking-wide text-white/80 transition-[translate] duration-300 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.215,0.61,0.355,1)] [font-size:max(var(--text-xs),0.5em)] group-hover:translate-y-0 group-hover:delay-200 group-hover:duration-600 motion-reduce:transition-none"><%= category %></span>
  </span>
</span>
